00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:17.660
I'm Valliappa Lakshmanan.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.250
Everyone calls me Lak.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.190
And this talk comes
about after having

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:25.370
talked to dozens of
customers over the years

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:29.240
in terms of basically answering
a very simple question.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.420
All right, I'm hearing
all of this hype about AI,

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.970
but where do I actually use it?

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:38.780
What kinds of use
cases come to mind?

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:40.650
And typically the
answer to this is,

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.740
you show them a
laundry list of, here's

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.050
a whole bunch of people
who have used it.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.550
And what I have
tried to do here is

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.650
to step back from
that a little bit

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:53.220
and come up with principles, the
kinds of things that you see,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.490
the very broad areas.

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:59.130
And we can basically look at it
from a couple of perspectives.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.340
One way is I'll
talk about how you

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.080
see these in Google products.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.400
And I'll also talk about
how we see these in terms

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.590
of our customer's use cases.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.120
So when I talk about AI,
and when you hear our AI,

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:18.170
you probably think of
things like Google Photos.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:20.510
You think of things
like Smart Reply.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.360
These are incredibly buzzworthy.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.330
You hear about image
models and sequence models.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:27.740
If you're a
technical person, you

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.980
think about convolutional neural
networks, and recurring neural

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.680
networks, and LSTMs, et cetera.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.200
But when you talk
about how do I use AI,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.750
I would encourage you to
forget about all of that buzz.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:49.180
Because ultimately, what
drives value in most companies

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.440
is machine learning on
structured data, data that

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.290
lives in a database, data that
lives in a data warehouse,

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.689
even at Google.

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:03.480
And this is something
that we did because--

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.340
it's possible to
do because you can

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.770
go through the entire
Google codebase

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:11.610
and look at all of the models
and see what kind of margins

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.290
are people using.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.560
An MLP is a
multilayer perceptron.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.800
It's essentially your
standard neural network

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.940
with nothing fancy in it,
just having a few layers.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.940
That is 60% of all
the models at Google.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.680
And this is the kind of model
that you use if you have a--

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.460
if you have data that is
just numeric and categorical,

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.700
just structured data.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.500
LSTMs are the kind
of model that you

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.030
use if you have time series
data or the kinds of models

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.550
that you use if you
have text data--

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.520
translation, text summarization.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.850
Smart Reply is an
example of an LSTM model.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:57.800
That is another 30% of
all the models at Google.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.850
So all of the
buzzworthy image models

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.880
that we hear about, that
every tutorial talks about,

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.800
that everybody puts
on their slide to say,

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.220
this is what you can do
with AI, that's less than 5%

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.890
of the models.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.180
So what do you focus
on when you say I

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.400
want to use AI in my business?

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.170
Structured data.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.140
So that's the first
thing to remember,

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.020
It's that the value
actually comes from the data

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.810
that you have in
your data warehouse.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:35.170
So how do we use structured
data for AI and then when we

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.840
talk about buzz again, what--

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.390
we talk about AI, and then we
talk about machine learning.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:44.070
And machine learning is the
part of AI that works today.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.880
So again, when we talk
about what do we do?

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.190
Machine learning, right?

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:50.380
So how do we use
machine learning?

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.920
It's essentially a way to
use standard algorithms

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.950
to get predictive
insights from data

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:58.790
to make repeated decisions.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.270
So let's break that
down a little bit.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:04.210
The first thing, of course,
is that machine learning only

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:08.020
works when you have data, and
preferably lots and lots of it.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:13.810
So you have the data, but unlike
the traditional, typical ways

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.779
that you use your data,
the difference here

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:19.839
is that the algorithms
that you apply to your data

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:21.310
are standard.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.560
So whether you're
a retail company,

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.570
or you're an oil
and gas company,

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:30.820
or your a media company, the
way that you take your data,

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.240
the algorithms that you apply
are relatively standard.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.140
And that's basically what
has driven this big use,

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.990
this big growth in
the applications

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.100
of machine learning.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:45.700
It's a fact that the
same kinds of models

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.310
work across industries,
work across verticals.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:53.350
So you apply these standard
algorithms to the data.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:56.390
But that's-- those standard
algorithms themselves

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:57.430
aren't new either.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.750
When you talk about
decision trees or you

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.680
about random forests, they've
been around for a while.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.070
And those are what I mean
by a standard algorithm.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.790
A decision tree is
a standard algorithm

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:10.460
that you can apply regardless
of which industry you're in.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:14.020
But the key thing is that you
apply these algorithms to data

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:16.570
to create predictive insights.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.740
Normally when we look at
data, what are we doing?

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.790
We are creating
backward-looking insights.

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:25.040
We're looking at your data and
you're saying, what happened?

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.030
You're trying to
understand what happened.

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:31.690
Machine learning is about
doing a predictive thing,

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.550
predicting something
that happened.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.440
But that's not it.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.130
Until this point, I could
be explaining everything

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:43.600
that a business analyst does
pretty much at every company.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.850
The big difference between
what a human analyst does

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.570
and what a machine
does is this last bit.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.820
It's a repeated decision bit.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.400
The idea is that you
don't apply machine

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:01.990
learning if you need to
make a decision once a year.

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.780
If you need to make a
decision once a month,

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.730
that is not an ML use case.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:10.780
An ML use case is
a decision that you

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.580
make over and over again,
many, many, many times a day.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.950
It's the kind of thing
that you do every time

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:18.970
a customer visits you.

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:21.520
If you want to determine,
is the shopping cart

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.039
going to be abandoned?

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:24.580
Now, that is a
decision that you have

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:29.470
to make for every customer
who visits your web page.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.600
If you're trying to
make a decision of,

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:36.750
should I place my new
store in this location?

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:40.461
That is a decision that you
may make 50 times a year.

00:06:40.461 --> 00:06:43.500
The 50-times-a-year decision
is not a good candidate

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.090
for machine learning.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:49.350
The thousands and millions
of decisions a day,

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.720
that is a great candidate
for machine learning.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.700
And somewhere in
between, you have

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.640
to make a judgment call of
whether this is a data analysis

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.060
task, or is it a
machine learning task.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:06.570
So, to give you an example of
where this idea comes about,

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.310
let me start with Google.

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:12.090
So our flagship application
is, of course search.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:13.510
We are a search company.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.390
And the way search used
to work a few years ago

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:19.150
was that you would go
into the Search bar,

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:21.700
and you would type in
the keyword Giants,

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:25.870
and we had to show you either
the San Francisco Giants, which

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.050
is a baseball team, or
the New York Giants, which

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:30.760
is a football team.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.710
Which one do we show you first?

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.780
Well, the way this
used to work was

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:39.860
that there was a rule base deep
in the bowels of the search

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:42.830
code that said if
the query is Giants,

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:45.230
and if the user is
located in the Bay Area,

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:48.410
show them results of the
San Francisco Giants.

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:50.770
If the user is in
New York, show them

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:52.500
results of a New York Giants.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.830
And the user or
somewhere else, show

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:57.780
them results about tall people.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:05.040
And that is basically the rule
base for that one word giants.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.740
Imagine how complex
the code base

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:15.160
gets for every single thing that
could have multiple meanings.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:16.620
So that's essentially
the problem

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.590
that your field is
facing because we

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:22.170
are writing hand-coded
rules like this for each

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.051
of these query phrases.

00:08:25.051 --> 00:08:27.980
And machine learning comes
about because we say,

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:31.730
this is a decision that we have
to make over and over again,

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:34.230
and we need to make it
lots and lots of times.

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:36.620
So how do we scale
this so that we

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:40.429
don't have to have
someone basically create

00:08:40.429 --> 00:08:44.400
this rule for every
single possible query

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.100
term that could exist?

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:51.730
And that's basically
what prompted RankBrain,

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:55.450
which is essentially a machine
learning algorithm that started

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:58.930
from this premise that, hey,
when someone comes to our web

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:01.710
page and they do a
query, we show them

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:05.410
a list of possible results,
we know which of them the

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:06.830
actually clicked on.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:10.120
So now we can train a
machine learning model

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:14.590
to basically predict what the
best possible result ought

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:17.890
to be for a specific query term.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.500
And we have enough
examples of this.

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:23.470
We don't have to go run
and create separate rules

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:25.720
for every one of
the query terms.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:29.360
We basically have a machine
learning model do this for us.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.260
So it's-- search is not
purely machine learning.

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:36.010
There are many, many,
many signals that we use.

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:39.550
But the machine learning
signal turned out

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:43.870
to basically create
the kind of improvement

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:48.460
that we see over two years
of work, that one signal did.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:49.630
And that was amazing.

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.750
And that basically was what
prompted Google to sit up

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:57.610
and say, man, this machine
learning thing, it has legs.

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.500
So what does that
essentially say?

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:00.740
So here's another example.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.480
This is of our customers.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.205
So Rolls-Royce-- basically,
you say Rolls-Royce,

00:10:05.205 --> 00:10:08.470
and you think cars, but
Rolls-Royce also does shipping.

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:12.700
And they needed to basically
interpret marine data sets.

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.550
And again, the way they would
do this was basically it

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:16.780
was a humongous data set.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.700
They needed lots
and lots of rules.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.700
And those rules had
to be crafted by hand.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:27.940
And they were basically able to
create safety measures simply

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:31.450
by-- basically, instead of doing
this rule creation by hand,

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:34.000
by basically taking
those marine data sets

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.300
and inferring what
kind of actions

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:41.000
ought to be taken
through machine learning.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:45.250
So the basic idea, the
first premise here, where--

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:47.080
how do you use AI?

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:50.080
How do you use machine learning
to leverage your business?

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:54.730
Number one, look back
into your business.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:59.140
Think about all the problems
for which you are creating rules

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:04.450
today, any problem for which
you're creating rules today.

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:08.080
And you have been
doing the good thing,

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:12.460
which is every time you've
made a rule-based decision,

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:14.860
you basically have a
data set that says,

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:18.330
this is the information
I use to basically--

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.130
the information that went
into your decision tree

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:23.980
to do the rule and the outcome.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:25.720
Was it actually correct or not?

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:29.590
If you identified fraud
using a bunch of rules,

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:33.460
was this transaction actually
fraudulent, or was it not?

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:35.330
So that's your label.

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:39.070
And if you've been doing
this rule-based, data-driven

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:42.580
decisions for a
period of time, that

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:44.980
becomes a machine
learning data set.

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:48.910
And you can basically get out
of this business of handcrafting

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:51.940
rules and basically turn it
over to a machine learning

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:55.810
model, which can
basically infer what

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:59.710
the rules ought to be, and to
do this in a more holistic way.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.140
So number one, how
do you basically

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:03.790
leverage AI in your cloud?

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:05.980
Look at all your
rule-based systems.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:07.480
Look at all your
rule based systems.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.550
Look at all the
systems, especially

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:11.650
for which you have been
collecting outcomes.

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:14.260
If you've not been collecting
the outcomes for the decisions

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.840
that you're making,
please start,

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:20.710
start saving whether those
decisions actually worked out

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:21.790
or didn't.

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:24.703
And use those as basically the
input to your machine learning

00:12:24.703 --> 00:12:25.550
models.

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:27.070
So that's number one.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:28.870
So let's take a second use case.

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:32.990
And in this case, I'll be
using Google Maps to illustrate

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:37.030
the machine learning journey,
the journey of a company that's

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:39.130
basically going from--

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:41.330
going through the machine
learning transformation.

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:43.300
So I use Google
Maps all the time.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:46.180
And I live somewhere in
the south of that diagram.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:49.977
And I work in Google Kirkland in
the north part of the diagram.

00:12:49.977 --> 00:12:50.560
And every day.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:53.890
I look at Maps, and
Maps gives me a route

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:58.310
to get from home to work.

00:12:58.310 --> 00:12:59.690
So great.

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:01.640
And I can look at that.

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:05.120
But is that machine learning?

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:08.930
And you could say, well, I
told Google where I live.

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:11.300
I told Google where I work.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:13.910
And this is basically the route.

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:18.650
And going from A to B, this
is Dijkstra's algorithm.

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:20.510
This is the A-star algorithm.

00:13:20.510 --> 00:13:23.360
We teach it in undergraduate
computer science classes.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.340
It's a deterministic algorithm.

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:27.290
So it's not machine learning.

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:29.530
Going from A to B is
not machine learning.

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:33.650
So, it is essentially
a deterministic rule.

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:37.460
It's a rule that you can
write down on paper and do it.

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:38.610
How about this?

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.770
So I was in Japan.

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:44.810
And I was in a subway
station called Roppongi.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:46.910
And Google Maps
essentially told me

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:50.180
that you're on floor number
two of Roppongi station.

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:52.610
And to basically
get to Google Japan,

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:55.220
this is a route that
you have to take.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:58.970
How does Google Maps know
that I'm on floor number two

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:01.150
of the subway station?

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:03.780
GPS-- I mean, this
is underground.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:06.630
I'm not going to get
altitude out of it.

00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:11.370
So at that point, the only
way to know where I am

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:16.740
is to basically use a whole
bunch of other data sources.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:20.040
But the point is to
get to that stage

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:21.750
where you're now
thinking about how

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:23.970
do I figure out
where the user is

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:26.310
from a variety of
different data sources,

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:29.250
you have to have solved
problem number one, which

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:33.390
is how to get from A to B. That
is the core of your business.

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:36.720
Basically provide navigation
information from A to B,

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:38.740
and then you're basically
then saying, OK,

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:43.260
now how do I use data, how
do I use ML models basically

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:45.300
improve that experience?

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:47.340
And that's what
that second bit is.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:51.030
It's about improving the
core business experience

00:14:51.030 --> 00:14:53.340
that you do with number one.

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.860
And then you get the
icing on the cake, which

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:59.230
is looking at a Google Now Card
that says, this is where I am,

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:01.780
what can I do in
between meetings?

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:05.700
And I was able to suggest
to me that I should go look

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:09.030
at the Santore Museum of Art,
which has the kind of paintings

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:10.055
that I like.

00:15:10.055 --> 00:15:12.180
Now, if you're a sports
fan, it might have probably

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:15.780
suggested to you a sumo
wrestling match close by.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:19.830
But the point being that this
is now completely personalized.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:24.480
And it's still in that
same realm of location,

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:27.990
and location-based
services and giving you

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:30.400
that additional information.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:36.450
So this is basically the journey
that enterprises go through

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:37.460
with machine learning.

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:39.420
You start with the
core of your business

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.520
that you typically solve
and you have already solved,

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:45.390
and then you look at
how to basically address

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:51.150
individual use cases
of that experience,

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:54.130
of that user experience
in a better way.

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:58.290
And then finally, you look
about how do I basically

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:04.140
reach that very long tail
of that very rare situations

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:08.010
that we need to do
very, very well in?

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:10.000
So let me take in
another example of this.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.870
This is in the case of, again,
talking about this journey

00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:15.820
to give you an idea
of how this works.

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:19.170
Let's say that we
want to basically--

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:21.120
we are a media
company, and we want

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:25.930
to find the best time
slot for a TV show.

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:27.560
So how do we do this?

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:31.270
Well, we might basically
have data that says,

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:34.600
OK, here's the
age and the number

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.850
of viewers of that
particular age

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:42.640
group who basically have viewed
shows like this show before.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:46.330
And I have location
information of people who have

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.640
viewed this show in the past.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.160
And then based on the
location information,

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:54.010
based on the demographic,
based on the desirability

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:56.660
of that demographic,
I would basically say,

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:58.930
OK, we're going to
take this new show,

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:01.950
and it looks like
7:30 PM on Wednesday

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:06.530
is the best time
slot for this show.

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:10.220
This is essentially your
traditional data analysis.

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:12.109
There is no machine
learning here yet.

00:17:12.109 --> 00:17:16.790
But in order to do this,
what data have you collected?

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:18.980
You've basically
collected a bunch of data

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:22.569
around the demographics of
people watching the show.

00:17:22.569 --> 00:17:27.050
You basically found the genre
of each of your different shows.

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:29.490
You know where the location
is of all of these people who

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:31.380
have been watching these shows.

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:33.740
So now given all of
this information,

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:37.910
can we do the next thing, the
second step of that journey?

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:39.780
What's the second
step of the journey?

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:42.180
Let's build a movie
recommendation system.

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:45.110
We might say, OK,
let's go ahead and say

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:47.540
let's build a movie
recommendation system.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:51.320
And your first approach,
the simple approach

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:52.670
would be something like this.

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:56.600
You would say, I'm going
to recommend popular movies

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:59.690
in the genre that
this user likes.

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:03.140
So if I have a user who's
between 30 and 39 years old,

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:07.340
he's male, makes between
$50,000 and $100,000,

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:10.670
and lives in South
Korea, and over time I

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:14.580
know that this person has
watched a romantic com--

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:17.960
like a romance, two comedies
followed by romance,

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:20.720
followed by a comedy, then
my prediction algorithm

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.810
might be, let me go
ahead and find the genre

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:25.940
that the user has
watched most often--

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:27.740
in this case, it's comedy--

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:30.240
and then let me go
find where they live--

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:31.610
which is South Korea--

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:37.160
and find the five most popular
comedy movies in South Korea.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:41.730
And that is my movie
recommendation for that user.

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:45.990
It's a pretty straightforward,
rule-based algorithm

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:49.350
that basically takes
advantage of the data.

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:52.490
But the thing is it relies on
the data that you already have,

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:56.400
this structured data that you
have off the history of things

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.130
that a person has watched
and something that you know

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:01.140
about this particular user.

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:04.710
How does this become
machine learning?

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:06.500
You flip this around.

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:08.570
Notice that we have
a model, I have data,

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.490
and I have prediction.

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:12.500
Instead, you start
from the data.

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:14.210
You don't start from
this idea that I

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:17.210
know what the model ought
to be, and therefore I'm

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:19.880
going to go to that data, I'm
going to make my prediction.

00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:22.520
Instead you start from the data.

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:24.560
You say, what's my data?

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:28.580
I have all the movies, and
I have all of the ratings

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:32.970
that every user has done
of all of the movies.

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:35.920
And then your model
is, if I'm going

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:38.100
to go ahead and do
a prediction, I'm

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:41.670
going to find all the
similar movies across all

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:45.960
the similar users using
criteria like the user's

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:49.540
current preference, and their
age, and a variety of things.

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:53.730
But notice here now, all
those very hard boundaries

00:19:53.730 --> 00:20:00.700
of age, income, genre, none
of those exist anymore.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:02.730
This is much more amorphous.

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:05.700
It can basically take into
account that people are not--

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:07.050
you can't bend them.

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:09.270
Instead, it's a very
continuous range.

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:11.520
And you're finding
similarity measures.

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:14.370
And that's basically what a
machine learning helps you do.

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:18.750
It helps you basically take
into account multiple factors

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:21.220
and weight them appropriately.

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:22.920
So this is essentially
that journey

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:26.820
that you go through
from forest--

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:31.230
basically taking your core
problem, solving it with data,

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:32.940
thinking about how
to take that data

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:36.540
and solve it with analytics,
and then using that data

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:40.110
and rethinking it in such a
way that you don't have to--

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:43.540
you can basically account
for different factors.

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:46.110
So in other words
then, the second thing

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:48.210
that you can do with
machine learning,

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:51.120
the second way that you leverage
your business of machine

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:53.850
learning, is that this
is how you personalize

00:20:53.850 --> 00:20:56.760
your applications, and
this is how you basically

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:02.220
reach the people who are unlike
every other 30 to 39-year-old

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:04.200
who lives in South Korea.

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:07.980
You're able to find
smaller groups of people

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:11.550
that they're like and give
them much more targeted

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:14.320
recommendations.

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:16.740
So machine learning
is about basically--

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:18.310
I think mostly
what we do when we

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:21.410
do businesses is that
we basically say,

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:25.631
we're going to take the middle
part of our distribution,

00:21:25.631 --> 00:21:27.130
and we're going to
target everything

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:29.800
towards the 80% of our users.

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:32.380
And we're going to forget
about the other 20% who

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.244
do crazy things.

00:21:34.244 --> 00:21:35.660
But instead, what
machine learning

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:38.770
lets you do is that it lets
you basically go walk down

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:42.190
into that tail, and
basically for every user find

00:21:42.190 --> 00:21:45.370
similar users and be able to
do recommendations for them.

00:21:48.730 --> 00:21:50.790
So to get to the
third thing I'll

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:53.620
start with one of my favorite
quotes is what Andrew Ng.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:55.780
Andrew Ng is, of
course, a famous machine

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:57.010
learning researcher.

00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:58.390
And you would
think that he would

00:21:58.390 --> 00:22:02.800
be the one who would be all gung
ho about the latest advances

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:04.810
in machine learning theory.

00:22:04.810 --> 00:22:09.550
But instead, Andrew says, "it's
not about the best algorithm.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.537
It's about who has
the most data."

00:22:11.537 --> 00:22:13.120
Because that's the
other thing that we

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:15.340
have learned over
and over again is

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:17.680
that the best machine
learning algorithms

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:22.100
are the ones that have access to
more data than any other ones.

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:25.217
So when you look-- when
you compare two algorithms,

00:22:25.217 --> 00:22:26.800
forget about what
everybody tells you.

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:30.260
If you have two products that
essentially do the same thing,

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:32.470
ask them what data
was this trained on.

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:34.810
If you have two image
models, ask them

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:36.670
what data was it trained on.

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:41.430
And if one model was trained
on more data and better data

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.305
than the other one, that--

00:22:43.305 --> 00:22:45.430
it doesn't matter what the
quality of the algorithm

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:49.930
is, that data is basically
going to control the quality

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:51.400
of the result that you get.

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:54.700
So inevitably it's about the
data, the quality of the data,

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:56.510
and the quantity of the data.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:59.770
So, what does that
have to do with the way

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:05.110
we approach our data
stewardship because everything

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:07.700
starts with data management.

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:10.990
If you think back about
how you collect your data--

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:13.900
and this is something that
I'm now noticing now--

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:19.640
is lots of times what we
do is that we take our data

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:23.210
and we aggregate
it, and we store it,

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:26.150
and we try to build
algorithms, machine learning

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:30.710
algorithms and data analysis
tasks, on the aggregated data.

00:23:30.710 --> 00:23:32.180
What do you mean by that?

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:34.760
Instead of doing
machine learning

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:38.320
on every individual
transaction, you're

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:43.370
try to forecast
sales by aggregating

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:47.360
all the transactions over
days and take the daily data

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:50.500
and use it to predict sales.

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:54.450
That doesn't work because
what have you just done?

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:57.300
You've taken this
very rich data source

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:00.780
that you have of every
individual transaction that

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:04.710
has ever happened, and
you've thrown it all away,

00:24:04.710 --> 00:24:07.050
and you've combined everything.

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:11.430
And now you basically have
a data set of 365 points.

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:14.250
And you're trying to use
that to predict sales.

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:15.540
Stop.

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:18.390
Don't train your
model on 365 points.

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:20.280
Go back to that
original data where you

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:22.200
had 80 million transactions.

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:29.220
You need to train your models
not on the filtered data, not

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:32.850
on the aggregated data,
but on the raw data,

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:35.460
on the original data.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:39.570
So that's-- machine learning is
about doing things on as much

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:41.340
data as you can.

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:44.220
Don't aggregate
things too early.

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:45.870
Don't filter things too early.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:47.952
Don't throw away data too early.

00:24:47.952 --> 00:24:50.160
That doesn't mean that you
shouldn't clean things up,

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:52.770
you should make sure
they're good quality.

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:54.690
We're saying that try
to get it to the point

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.610
where you don't need
to throw away data,

00:24:56.610 --> 00:24:59.280
that you don't need to
aggregate this data to reduce

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:03.120
the noise in it, that each
individual transaction data has

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:06.090
the right information that
you need to build a better

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:08.850
machine learning model.

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:11.980
But it's not just about
the data that you have.

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:15.310
Also think about all
the ancillary things

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:17.390
that affect your business.

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:19.180
So it's not just about
the data that you

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:22.490
have in your data warehouse,
think about weather data,

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:25.630
think about traffic data,
think about political events.

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:28.060
All these things that
affect your business, so

00:25:28.060 --> 00:25:32.380
think about how to basically
join your data sets but all

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:35.020
these other diverse factors.

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:37.300
And that is the
data set that you

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:40.060
should be using to build
your machine learning models,

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:41.380
to build your AI systems.

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:48.880
And then, having now
taken your data warehouse,

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:51.730
considered all the third
party and partner information

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:56.420
that you can join with, ask
yourself a third question.

00:25:56.420 --> 00:26:00.600
What data could I be collecting
that I'm not collecting today?

00:26:03.610 --> 00:26:06.130
Sensors are getting
really cheap.

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:09.410
There are something
like $8 billion devices

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:13.540
that came online last year.

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:15.640
It's just an astounding number.

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:17.540
And this is just
connected devices.

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:19.450
I'm not talking
about devices that

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:20.950
are not permanently connected.

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:23.020
These are just
connected devices.

00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:26.680
And they're-- it's getting
to the point where data is

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:28.750
ubiquitous, and you can get it.

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:32.440
The question is, are you
collecting the data that

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:34.120
actually impacts your business?

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:36.610
So make sure that you
have a data strategy that

00:26:36.610 --> 00:26:39.220
involves collecting this
data from as many sources

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:42.640
as you can, whether their
phones, or their IoT devices,

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:43.960
or whatever.

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:47.380
And then, think about
how to basically take

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:49.210
that big data that
you now have--

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:51.700
and what I mean here
by big data is not

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:54.670
the quantity of the data,
unaggregated, unfiltered,

00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:57.790
the raw data joined with
everything else that

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:01.315
affects you with streaming
data that's coming in.

00:27:01.315 --> 00:27:04.570
In the old days, like the
three V's in other words--

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:07.960
volume, variety, and velocity.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:09.669
So that's basically
what I mean here.

00:27:09.669 --> 00:27:11.960
And that's actually-- when
you think about it that way,

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:15.595
and you thoughtfully consider
all of these things that affect

00:27:15.595 --> 00:27:19.300
it, it's changing businesses.

00:27:19.300 --> 00:27:22.210
Just take the first one, games.

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:25.570
10 years ago, games
were board games.

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:30.330
Now games are essentially
completely customized

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:32.710
to the person playing it.

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:34.480
The characters
that you encounter

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:36.970
when you play a game
are very different

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:39.460
and have different
skills than the character

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:41.320
that somebody else encounters.

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:43.720
And the only reason
that that is possible

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:46.840
is because of the amount of
data that gaming companies are

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:49.570
able to collect, and how
they're able to customize

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:52.500
the game as you play the game.

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:55.600
And you see this over and
over again as you walk down

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:59.380
that list of companies that are
getting completely transforming

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:02.320
by rethinking the data
that they're collecting

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:05.150
and what they can
do with the data.

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:08.680
So as an example of
another one, Schlumberger,

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:10.930
who's one of JCP's
customers, talking

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:14.560
about how Cloud IoT Core allowed
them to focus their engineering

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:18.810
efforts to basically build
reliable economic things.

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:23.140
They're talking about 30
terabytes of petrochemical data

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:26.470
that they can now use
to build their models.

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:31.880
So that's basically the scale
that you should be thinking at.

00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:36.270
So the third aspect I
want to leave you with

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:39.830
is this idea that when you're
designing your systems,

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:43.760
design them with the
expectation that next year you

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:45.180
will have more data.

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:47.180
Don't start with
this defeatist idea

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:50.330
that the data that you
have is all the data

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:52.010
that you have today.

00:28:52.010 --> 00:28:54.710
You will be able to get
more data next year,

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:57.500
and you want to basically
work towards getting that more

00:28:57.500 --> 00:29:00.560
data, more diverse data,
and build your models

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:02.390
to account for that.

00:29:02.390 --> 00:29:06.910
So that's the third aspect of
this, is design for more data,

00:29:06.910 --> 00:29:09.460
for unfiltered,
unaggregated data.

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:14.550
To get another idea
of just talking

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:16.470
about the kind of
devices, and being

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:18.660
able to connect
them, and basically

00:29:18.660 --> 00:29:22.740
create new businesses that just
didn't exist a few years ago,

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:25.920
many of us use things
like Philips Hue,

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:30.480
basically allows us to change
the way lighting works.

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:33.410
And now Phillips
basically processes

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:37.450
25 million remote
lighting commands a day,

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:38.775
something that--

00:29:38.775 --> 00:29:42.720
a thing that did not
exist three years ago.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:44.780
And it's just possible
because of all

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:47.750
of these trends that are
connecting together--

00:29:47.750 --> 00:29:51.860
data, connected devices, the
ability to basically train

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:55.250
your models and basically
deal with unstructured data

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:58.630
like lighting commands.

00:29:58.630 --> 00:30:02.590
But this is all great, but
what happens when you collect

00:30:02.590 --> 00:30:04.390
petabytes and exabytes of data?

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:12.240
If you're going to be
spending all of your time

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:17.150
doing provisioning,
worrying about reliability,

00:30:17.150 --> 00:30:19.930
worrying about handling
the growing scale,

00:30:19.930 --> 00:30:23.210
if you're going to be
worrying about utilization,

00:30:23.210 --> 00:30:26.760
you're not going to be deriving
value from any of this data.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:31.100
You're going to be spending
all of your head count, all

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:33.810
of your human resources.

00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:37.670
All of the engineers are
going to be basically just

00:30:37.670 --> 00:30:40.800
working on keeping this data up.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:42.860
And that's not what
you want to be doing.

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:45.410
You want to basically
be deriving data,

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:46.820
deriving value from it.

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:48.530
You want to understand the data.

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:51.890
And this is basically
something that we at Google

00:30:51.890 --> 00:30:53.630
have a lot of experience with.

00:30:53.630 --> 00:30:55.630
And this is kind of
why we've talked--

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:59.360
we've been basically building
serverless data analysis,

00:30:59.360 --> 00:31:03.860
serverless ETL tools, serverless
machine learning tools.

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:07.460
The reason that pretty much when
you look at our data platform,

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:09.203
everything is serverless.

00:31:09.203 --> 00:31:11.930
Everything is a
fully-managed service

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:16.430
is simply because there is
no way to do both things.

00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:20.720
There is no way to both have
an engineer both be worried

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:24.020
about is the data going to
be there when I need it,

00:31:24.020 --> 00:31:27.920
and being able to
collect and basically be

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:31.550
able to create models to
derive value from that data.

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:34.820
So even though I
think we started out

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:39.200
talking about MapReduce and
like the equivalent of these

00:31:39.200 --> 00:31:44.300
in 2004, we quickly realized
that that cluster-centric way

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:46.940
of thinking wasn't
going to scale.

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:48.920
And we basically
moved on to things

00:31:48.920 --> 00:31:52.430
like Dataflow, which is open
source to the Apache Beam,

00:31:52.430 --> 00:31:55.370
to basically deal with this idea
that you want to do serverless

00:31:55.370 --> 00:31:58.640
ETL, serverless extract
transform load, build

00:31:58.640 --> 00:31:59.730
pipelines.

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:02.570
So don't think of
serverless as purely being

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:05.030
around low-level functions.

00:32:05.030 --> 00:32:08.180
Serverless should be about
your entire workflow.

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:11.190
Your entire workflow
needs to be serverless.

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:15.740
And that's something that-- that
is the way that you basically

00:32:15.740 --> 00:32:19.070
manage petabytes and
exabytes of data.

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:20.450
If you've used
BigQuery, you know

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:22.000
exactly how we think about it.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:24.170
You think of-- you
write your SQL code,

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:25.940
and you don't worry
about the thousands

00:32:25.940 --> 00:32:29.120
of slots that come up
to basically execute

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:30.620
those queries for you.

00:32:30.620 --> 00:32:32.120
That is how you
should be thinking.

00:32:32.120 --> 00:32:36.500
You should be thinking in terms
of code not infrastructure.

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:38.720
So and this is something that--

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:41.240
BigQuery is one of those
things that people immediately

00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:42.410
get when they see it.

00:32:42.410 --> 00:32:44.960
And it drives a lot
of transformation

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:46.580
and a lot of our customers.

00:32:46.580 --> 00:32:49.670
AirAsia, for example,
said, hey, we just

00:32:49.670 --> 00:32:54.950
need a platform that
can scale to basically

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:59.060
have our appetite for just
this amazing growth in data

00:32:59.060 --> 00:33:00.580
that they're seeing.

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:03.830
And BigQuery was
ideal for that task.

00:33:03.830 --> 00:33:07.070
So when you have this
increasing volumes of data,

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:10.950
and you need to continue
deriving value from it,

00:33:10.950 --> 00:33:14.670
think about fully-managed,
serverless solutions.

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:17.550
Don't settle for anything
that involves you

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:21.120
having to spin up a cluster.

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:25.710
So the idea is spend time
on what you need to do.

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:28.660
Don't spend time
on how to do it.

00:33:28.660 --> 00:33:31.980
So you focus on insight,
not infrastructure.

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:35.370
So another example of this is
Blue Apron, who talks about

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:38.100
after we moved to
BigQuery, or query time

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:40.080
was reduced exponentially.

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:44.340
So they were able to do a lot
more queries, and importantly,

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:47.080
accelerate the decision making.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:52.645
An extra day is very,
very, very valuable.

00:33:52.645 --> 00:33:54.770
And then, of course, the
other question that we get

00:33:54.770 --> 00:33:56.840
is, but I have lots of data.

00:33:56.840 --> 00:34:01.010
Can I really stop managing
all of this myself?

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:05.449
And my favorite anecdote
here is Evernote

00:34:05.449 --> 00:34:07.490
because this is done by
our professional services

00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:10.760
team, migrated three and
1/2 petabytes of content

00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:13.190
in just 70 days.

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:17.300
And all of these are
just very small documents

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:19.250
owned by millions of users.

00:34:19.250 --> 00:34:20.730
And we're able to do that.

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:22.790
And so it's definitely possible.

00:34:22.790 --> 00:34:24.230
You can move fast.

00:34:24.230 --> 00:34:26.540
And you can basically
get out of this

00:34:26.540 --> 00:34:28.730
managing your own
infrastructure business.

00:34:28.730 --> 00:34:33.900
So 4a, use a platform
that lets you

00:34:33.900 --> 00:34:36.810
forget about infrastructure.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:39.510
If there's a 4a,
there's got to be a 4b.

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:40.500
And what is that?

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:42.880
So this is about the data part.

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:45.510
But there's another bit
that any cloud platform

00:34:45.510 --> 00:34:49.260
has to give you if you want
to be successful in AI.

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:52.290
And it is that you
need flexibility.

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:56.699
You need to have the ability
to basically do your machine

00:34:56.699 --> 00:34:59.160
learning runtimes for
different scenarios.

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:00.630
For example, on
Google Cloud, you

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:04.710
can prototype with Cloud
Datalab or Deep Learning Image.

00:35:04.710 --> 00:35:08.550
You can do this prototype
locally on your little laptop

00:35:08.550 --> 00:35:12.840
and then run it
on-prem with Kubeflow

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:15.810
and migrate it over
time to ML Engine.

00:35:15.810 --> 00:35:18.850
So it's giving you this
for different scenarios.

00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:21.990
For example, if you have
a device out in the field,

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:25.311
and you want to be able to
run a model on that device,

00:35:25.311 --> 00:35:27.060
you want to make sure
that your models are

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:30.000
portable such that you
can train on the cloud,

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:34.080
but do the prediction on
the device out in the field.

00:35:34.080 --> 00:35:37.680
So you want to make
sure that any system

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:41.880
that you basically get and
you run your machine learning

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:45.050
runtimes on, this support all
these different scenarios,

00:35:45.050 --> 00:35:47.790
training on the cloud,
predicting on the device,

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:51.760
training on the
device, et cetera.

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:55.450
The other thing to realize
is that deep learning only

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:58.620
works because our
data sets are large.

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:01.750
So the graph on
the left basically

00:36:01.750 --> 00:36:04.060
talks about the
training data size.

00:36:04.060 --> 00:36:05.470
This is for a specific problem.

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:08.120
But different problems , you
see the exact same thing.

00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:11.740
So for those of you in the
back who can't quite see it,

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:16.430
the x-axis has 2 part 20, 2
part 21, 2 part 22, et cetera.

00:36:16.430 --> 00:36:20.980
So each grid is a
doubling of the data size.

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:24.730
And each time your data size
doubles, your error rate

00:36:24.730 --> 00:36:28.000
drops linearly.

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:30.370
So remember that
to drop your error

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:35.680
rate by x, you're basically--
your data set basically

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:38.840
has to go to a power of x.

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:42.130
Your data set doubles to get
to linear improvement in error

00:36:42.130 --> 00:36:42.650
rate.

00:36:42.650 --> 00:36:44.710
So it only works--

00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:48.020
this drop in error, it
happens with more data.

00:36:48.020 --> 00:36:49.720
But when we talk
about more data,

00:36:49.720 --> 00:36:51.820
we're not talking
10% more data, we're

00:36:51.820 --> 00:36:53.920
not talking 20% more data.

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:57.310
We're talking 1,000 times more
data, a million times more

00:36:57.310 --> 00:36:58.090
data.

00:36:58.090 --> 00:37:01.551
We're talking powers here,
lots and lots more data.

00:37:01.551 --> 00:37:03.550
So that's the one thing
that we want to remember

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:04.880
on the left-hand side.

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:09.290
The right-hand side is the
other scary part of the curve,

00:37:09.290 --> 00:37:11.440
which is these are the
machine learning models,

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:14.320
the state-of-the-art
machine learning models.

00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:18.260
And the x-axis
there is the year.

00:37:18.260 --> 00:37:22.770
And the y-axis is the compute
capability that you need,

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:26.380
petaFLOPS per day of training.

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:30.809
So AlexNet, which is on the
way left-hand side, that

00:37:30.809 --> 00:37:33.100
is the thing that essentially
started the deep learning

00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:37.330
revolution, is way down
in the corner there.

00:37:37.330 --> 00:37:40.510
And again, the y-axis
there is logarithmic.

00:37:40.510 --> 00:37:43.480
So every time a model
goes up in that grid,

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:48.490
the compute power is going 10
times, 100 times, 1,000 times.

00:37:48.490 --> 00:37:51.460
For now, look at
the compute power

00:37:51.460 --> 00:37:56.430
change the last six
years, from 10,000

00:37:56.430 --> 00:38:02.610
on the top y-axis,
0.0001 on the bottom.

00:38:02.610 --> 00:38:07.140
In other words, your compute
power that's needed to do

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:11.160
state-of-the-art machine
learning went up 10 million

00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:14.710
times in the last six years.

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:21.190
Just let that settle
in for a minute.

00:38:21.190 --> 00:38:26.140
The amount of data that you
need doubles, four times,

00:38:26.140 --> 00:38:28.030
quadruples.

00:38:28.030 --> 00:38:29.210
It goes in power.

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:31.930
The data requirement
goes in powers.

00:38:31.930 --> 00:38:34.610
The model requirement
goes in powers.

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:39.830
So you basically have
two power laws going on.

00:38:39.830 --> 00:38:43.340
And this is kind of why
your traditional chips,

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:47.620
architecture, Moore's law,
they don't really work anymore.

00:38:47.620 --> 00:38:51.560
That is-- this is the thing
that our leadership saw

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:55.100
three or four years ago that
prompted us to start developing

00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:59.720
the TPUs, basically this
need to completely change

00:38:59.720 --> 00:39:04.430
the game in terms of what kind
of data you need to handle

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:08.460
and what kind of computer you
need to be able to do this.

00:39:08.460 --> 00:39:11.990
So, if you want to
do machines earning,

00:39:11.990 --> 00:39:15.680
you need efficient,
cost-effective places

00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:17.520
to do machine learning.

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:20.450
So when we offer that, we offer
Compute Engine to which you

00:39:20.450 --> 00:39:25.020
can basically attach
CPUs, GPUs, or TPUs,

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:28.340
Kubeflow, which gives you
this hybrid environment,

00:39:28.340 --> 00:39:30.680
and Kubernetes Engine, which
gives you a great place

00:39:30.680 --> 00:39:34.280
to run it on Google Cloud,
and Cloud ML Engine, which

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:36.500
gives you a
fully-managed serverless

00:39:36.500 --> 00:39:39.860
way they use all
of this hardware,

00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:42.200
and to basically do this
in a distributed way.

00:39:42.200 --> 00:39:45.920
So the way you handle the
increasing amounts of data

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:47.750
is distribution.

00:39:47.750 --> 00:39:51.020
And the way you handle the
increasing amounts of compute

00:39:51.020 --> 00:39:54.020
is better and better
hardware chips.

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:57.380
So you need distributed
hardware chips.

00:39:57.380 --> 00:40:00.271
And that's basically what
something like ML Engine

00:40:00.271 --> 00:40:00.770
gives you.

00:40:00.770 --> 00:40:02.930
It gives you distribution,
and it gives you

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:04.190
the best of the breed chips.

00:40:04.190 --> 00:40:06.410
You need both of
those in order to do

00:40:06.410 --> 00:40:09.020
machine learning successfully.

00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:10.580
And the other
thing to realize is

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:13.910
that machine learning is moving
towards increasing levels of ML

00:40:13.910 --> 00:40:14.870
abstraction.

00:40:14.870 --> 00:40:16.430
Sorry, you did not seenit.

00:40:16.430 --> 00:40:19.070
I guess it's seenit,
so you already seenit.

00:40:19.070 --> 00:40:23.085
So, let me just start with
three years ago what happened.

00:40:23.085 --> 00:40:26.420
AUCNET is a Japanese
auction company.

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:28.370
And they wanted to
basically-- the way

00:40:28.370 --> 00:40:31.970
they want to rethink the
way car auctions work.

00:40:31.970 --> 00:40:34.580
The way it used to work was
that you wanted to sell a car,

00:40:34.580 --> 00:40:36.050
and you would
basically go fill out

00:40:36.050 --> 00:40:39.470
a form that said what car
you had, how old was it,

00:40:39.470 --> 00:40:41.510
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:40:41.510 --> 00:40:42.800
Filling out a form--

00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:43.980
boring.

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:46.430
So what AUCNET did was they
said, oh, just go around

00:40:46.430 --> 00:40:49.130
your car, take a bunch of
photographs of your car,

00:40:49.130 --> 00:40:51.560
upload those photographs
to our cloud,

00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:54.810
and we'll tell you how
much your car is worth.

00:40:54.810 --> 00:41:00.300
Imagine the friction
between filling out a form,

00:41:00.300 --> 00:41:02.940
take photos of your car.

00:41:02.940 --> 00:41:05.050
Completely changed the business.

00:41:05.050 --> 00:41:06.810
But in order to
do that, they had

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:11.380
to build a custom image model
so they could price the cars.

00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:12.330
They had to do it.

00:41:12.330 --> 00:41:15.570
They had to write the
tensorflow code themselves.

00:41:15.570 --> 00:41:16.860
We helped them do it.

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:17.610
They wrote it.

00:41:17.610 --> 00:41:18.394
It worked.

00:41:18.394 --> 00:41:19.560
They changed their business.

00:41:19.560 --> 00:41:20.760
Great.

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:23.170
But I'm talking about increasing
levels of abstraction.

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:27.030
So AUCNET wrote tensorflow code.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:29.710
Next level up, Ocado.

00:41:29.710 --> 00:41:32.250
Ocado is a UK grocer.

00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:35.880
They wanted to basically
handle customer service emails.

00:41:35.880 --> 00:41:38.640
The way it used to work was
a customer sends an email,

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:40.680
somebody at Ocado
reads the email, says,

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:44.550
oh, this customer is
basically saying something

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:46.500
about our produce.

00:41:46.500 --> 00:41:48.690
And then they forward to
the produce department,

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:50.356
and the person in the
produce department

00:41:50.356 --> 00:41:52.870
reads it and decides
what to do about it,

00:41:52.870 --> 00:41:54.690
it's a pretty wasteful process.

00:41:54.690 --> 00:41:55.740
So what did Ocado do?

00:41:55.740 --> 00:42:01.240
They used the Natural Language
API, a Google API built

00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:04.630
on Google data,
works out of the box,

00:42:04.630 --> 00:42:09.190
but it's not going to basically
identify very specific things

00:42:09.190 --> 00:42:10.957
that are Ocado-specific.

00:42:10.957 --> 00:42:12.790
But it's going to
basically say the customer

00:42:12.790 --> 00:42:14.680
is happy customer is
not happy, they're

00:42:14.680 --> 00:42:17.680
talking about
produce, et cetera.

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:21.670
And then Ocado basically use
that NLP API and then built

00:42:21.670 --> 00:42:23.740
a second model that
basically said,

00:42:23.740 --> 00:42:27.730
if the NLP API gives you the
tags, which department should

00:42:27.730 --> 00:42:29.630
you send it to?

00:42:29.630 --> 00:42:32.620
So in other words, they did
not have to go to the raw text

00:42:32.620 --> 00:42:35.650
and build a complete
model themselves.

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:38.950
They could basically
build off of the NLP API

00:42:38.950 --> 00:42:40.940
and build a smaller,
easier model.

00:42:43.670 --> 00:42:46.590
That is that abstraction
level that I'm talking about.

00:42:46.590 --> 00:42:49.640
You don't-- as
technology is improved,

00:42:49.640 --> 00:42:52.340
you increasingly don't have
to go down to this level

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:56.330
of dealing with raw
images, raw text.

00:42:56.330 --> 00:43:00.860
You can basically build
on top of existing APIs.

00:43:00.860 --> 00:43:05.010
Meanwhile, seenit wanted
to basically have--

00:43:05.010 --> 00:43:08.540
it allows people to
basically upload content.

00:43:08.540 --> 00:43:11.870
And sometimes people upload
inappropriate content

00:43:11.870 --> 00:43:13.130
for whatever reason.

00:43:13.130 --> 00:43:14.840
And so seenit
wants to reject it.

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:18.450
And they could rejected
using the Vision API--

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:21.310
straightforward,
just use it as is.

00:43:21.310 --> 00:43:28.570
And then the next level up,
Uniqlo, a Japanese retailer,

00:43:28.570 --> 00:43:31.750
basically need-- wanted to
basically create a chatbot.

00:43:31.750 --> 00:43:35.440
And again, they don't need to
go down to programming each

00:43:35.440 --> 00:43:36.940
of the individual
words, and what

00:43:36.940 --> 00:43:38.640
they mean in conversations.

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:40.930
They could basically
write in tense,

00:43:40.930 --> 00:43:43.030
this is what a
conversation is when

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:45.580
somebody wants to
find a matching blouse

00:43:45.580 --> 00:43:47.740
for their shirt.

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:50.320
And the chatbot
is basically able

00:43:50.320 --> 00:43:53.920
to take that and create
a realistic experience

00:43:53.920 --> 00:43:55.210
for the user.

00:43:55.210 --> 00:44:00.820
So you can think in terms of
not very low-level LSTM models,

00:44:00.820 --> 00:44:03.880
but at very high level, what
is the customer's intent?

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:06.290
What is a typical
transaction that they're

00:44:06.290 --> 00:44:07.540
going to walk through?

00:44:07.540 --> 00:44:10.480
And this is how you do it.

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:14.140
So, if you are doing ML,
what does this mean to you?

00:44:14.140 --> 00:44:17.160
It means that you
want to basically pick

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:23.640
a framework that lets you do at
every level of that abstraction

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:24.750
hierarchy.

00:44:24.750 --> 00:44:28.590
You want to build a
low-level tensorflow model,

00:44:28.590 --> 00:44:31.510
completely custom, no problem.

00:44:31.510 --> 00:44:34.330
Use ML Engine.

00:44:34.330 --> 00:44:38.440
If you want to basically use
out-of-the-box models that are

00:44:38.440 --> 00:44:42.400
basically trained on massive
amounts of data, no problem.

00:44:42.400 --> 00:44:45.970
Use a Vision API, the
Translate API, the Speech API.

00:44:45.970 --> 00:44:48.680
You have something
somewhere in the middle,

00:44:48.680 --> 00:44:51.130
you want to basically take
advantage of everything

00:44:51.130 --> 00:44:54.030
that the Vision API gives
you, but customize it

00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:55.830
to your own things?

00:44:55.830 --> 00:44:56.860
Use AutoML.

00:44:56.860 --> 00:45:00.100
You heard in that keynote today
that AutoML is no longer just

00:45:00.100 --> 00:45:01.120
about vision.

00:45:01.120 --> 00:45:05.740
We also text classification
in AutoML, the idea being we

00:45:05.740 --> 00:45:09.700
can basically build
on top of Google here.

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:11.740
So, that is 4b.

00:45:11.740 --> 00:45:15.850
When you think about how do I do
machine learning on the cloud,

00:45:15.850 --> 00:45:19.450
realize that machine learning,
in spite of all the hype,

00:45:19.450 --> 00:45:22.420
is ultimately is still software.

00:45:22.420 --> 00:45:25.300
And you want to make a
buy versus build decision.

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:27.610
And you want to make
it based on quality,

00:45:27.610 --> 00:45:30.910
based on the kinds of things
that you want to be able to do.

00:45:30.910 --> 00:45:34.133
So with that, we'll
kind of summarize.

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:45.580
So number one,
machine learning can

00:45:45.580 --> 00:45:48.430
be used to solve many problems
for which you're writing rules

00:45:48.430 --> 00:45:49.730
today.

00:45:49.730 --> 00:45:52.329
Number two, machine learning
is how you personalize

00:45:52.329 --> 00:45:53.245
all your applications.

00:45:53.245 --> 00:45:56.950
This is how you
reach the long tail.

00:45:56.950 --> 00:46:01.240
Number three, design your
systems with this expectation

00:46:01.240 --> 00:46:04.030
that you'll have a lot
more data next year.

00:46:04.030 --> 00:46:06.730
And number four-- I've
combined the a and b here--

00:46:06.730 --> 00:46:10.600
use a platform that lets you
forget about infrastructure

00:46:10.600 --> 00:46:14.230
that gives you a lot of
great pre-built models.

00:46:14.230 --> 00:46:15.730
Thank you.

00:46:15.730 --> 00:46:17.530
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:21.130 --> 00:46:24.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

